.TH KILL 1 "September 19, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
kill \- signal processes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kill
.RB [ \-signo " | " \-s " name" ]
.I pid ...
.br
.B kill \-l
.SH DESCRIPTION
Signals the specified processes.
Process numbers can be found by using ps(1).
The default signal is TERM (15).
A signal number preceded by - selects another signal;
-s accepts a symbolic name, without regard to case.
.PP
The names are HUP (1), INT (2), QUIT (3), KILL (9) and TERM (15).
The -l form lists these names.
Signal 0 probes whether the process is alive without signalling it.
.PP
Normally a nonzero signal terminates its target.
A process may ignore a signal or arrange to receive it at a safe point;
the shell's trap command uses this facility.
KILL cannot be caught or ignored.
A successful send does not imply that a caught or ignored signal killed
the process.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/KILL  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
ps(1), sh(1), licenses(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 if all operations succeed, 1 if any target cannot be signalled, 2 for usage errors.
.SH BUGS
This is a positive-PID interface, not Unix process-group signalling. Root-tier processes (init and the idle process) cannot be killed through this command. An interactive shell ignores TERM, as in V7 (see sh(1)); kill -9 kills the console shell, and init then starts a new one.
